# Pipeline Orchestrator

Manages the pipeline state machine. Derives state from git/gh, surfaces status on session start, and
auto-advances between gates in autopilot mode.

## Workflows

Two workflows. Build something or respond to review.

### build
Entry: `crew spec <issue>` Stages: spec → implement → review → commit → open-pr Terminal: PR opened
as draft Note: crew-specwriter infers the spec template (standard or bug) from the request content.
No `start bug` command — template selection is automatic.

### respond
Entry: `crew resolve PR <PR>` Stages: address-pr → commit → push Terminal: all review threads
resolved and pushed; reviewer approval is external

> **Lightweight path (no spec needed):** For changes touching ≤3 files with no new exports or API
> surface — `crew quick` (implement inline → `crew review changes`) → `crew commit`. Skip the spec,
> worktree, and task state entirely. Use full `build` when the change crosses that threshold.

## State machine

```
DRAFTING → [gate: approve spec] → APPROVED → IMPLEMENTING → DONE
```

Four states. Review, commit and the PR are steps inside `IMPLEMENTING`; their results are evidence,
not states. `DONE` is set when `crew open pr` creates the PR. Side states: `ON-HOLD` (auto after 14
idle days) and `ABANDONED` (set by the user only).

| From | To | Gate? | What runs |
|---|---|---|---|
| DRAFTING | APPROVED | **Gate 1** — user approves spec | `crew-ls.py --mark-approved` (see `crew-specwriter` Step 6) |
| APPROVED | IMPLEMENTING | auto | crew-implement skill (builder only); `crew-ls.py --mark-implementing` |
| IMPLEMENTING | DONE | **Gate 2** — user approves the PR title, description, base, and target | crew-open-pr skill, then `crew-ls.py --mark-done` |

Inside `IMPLEMENTING`, `crew review` and `crew commit` run as separate steps. Gate 2 is the PR
approval in `crew-open-pr`: the title, description, base, and target repo, shown before the push.
The commit message is reviewed in `crew-commit` (auto-committed when unambiguous, paused when not),
but approving a commit message is not the gate — Gate 2 is the push and PR creation.

Automatic transitions are data in `.agent/tools/transitions.json`, applied by
`crew-ls.py --reconcile --apply`. `DRAFTING|APPROVED` go `ON-HOLD` after 14 idle days, with the prior state recorded in the
status; resume by editing the Status back to the `was` value. `IMPLEMENTING` specs are reported as
ready to open a PR (all criteria checked, review pass or warn) or stalled, never rewritten. `DONE`
is written only by `crew-ls.py --mark-done` from `crew open pr`, and means the draft PR was opened,
not merged.

To show task state in chat, run `crew-ls.py --md` and paste its output as written, outside a code
fence, so it renders as a table.

## Session-start awareness

On every conversation start, check for an active task:

```bash
if [ -n "${CREW_TASK_DIR+x}" ]; then
  TASK_DIR="$CREW_TASK_DIR"
else
  TASK_DIR=$(~/.agent/bin/resolve-task-dir.py 2>/tmp/resolve-task-dir-err)
  EXIT_CODE=$?
  if [ $EXIT_CODE -ne 0 ]; then
    # Script ran but failed (git error, malformed .task-id, etc.) — surface it
    TASK_DIR=""
    echo "Warning: resolve-task-dir failed (exit $EXIT_CODE): $(cat /tmp/resolve-task-dir-err)" >&2
  else
    export CREW_TASK_DIR="$TASK_DIR"
  fi
fi
```

- If `$TASK_DIR` is non-empty: read SPEC.md slug, derive state, surface: `Active task: **<slug>**
  (<STATE>). Next: \`<next crew command>\`.` If `$TASK_DIR/HANDOFF.md` exists, append: `Handoff
  saved — run \`crew handoff resume\`.`
- If `$TASK_DIR` is empty and exit was 0: no active task — say nothing.
- If exit was non-zero: surface the warning above and say nothing further.

> **Known limitation:** `CREW_TASK_DIR` is session-scoped. If you switch branches mid-session (`git
> checkout`), the cached value points to the old task. Unset it manually — `unset CREW_TASK_DIR` —
> before running crew commands on the new branch.

## PROGRESS.md

Append-only event log. Never use it for gate or status decisions — SPEC.md plus git/gh are the only
state source. Skills may read it for narrative resume context (which tasks completed in an earlier
session). Format:
```
[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] TRANSITION: APPROVED → IMPLEMENTING
[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] TRANSITION: IMPLEMENTING → DONE (PR opened: <url>)
```

## Invariants

- Never start `build` implementation without an approved SPEC.md; the lightweight path is the only
  exception
- Done = acceptance criteria pass, not "looks right"
- **Git/gh is the source of truth for pipeline state** — PROGRESS.md is the audit trail
- Autopilot never skips a gate — it chains between gates, not through them
- Autopilot pauses and asks, rather than proceeding, when a step has an open question, expands or
  changes the approved scope (or user request when no spec exists), changes a public contract, or is
  hard to reverse (`personal.mdc` "Ask before acting on uncertainty")
- Autopilot never auto-retries on failure — the user always decides
- **Autopilot reads skill files directly — never re-triggers through the routing layer**

## METRIC lines (offline analysis only)

`METRIC:` lines are append-only audit records for offline analysis. `resolve-task-dir.py` must not
parse them for gates or transitions — git/gh plus SPEC.md remain the source of truth.

- Format: `[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS] METRIC: <event> [<key=value> ...]`
- Write with `~/.agent/bin/log-metric.py "$TASK_DIR" <event> [key=value ...]`
- Events: `implement_start mode=inline`, `implement_complete tasks_passed=N tasks_failed=N`,
 `commit sha=<short>`, `review verdict=pass|block`, `completion_protocol phase=ac_check`,
 `session tool_calls=N`

See `.agent/tools/TOOLS.md` for the full contract.
